```{r}
library(ggplot2)
```

> Q1. With the help of the R “addin” package datapasta assign the CDC pertussis case number data to a data frame called cdc and use ggplot to make a plot of cases numbers over time.

```{r}
ggplot(cdc) +
  aes(Year, Cases) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  labs(x="Year", y="Cases")
```

> Q2. Using the ggplot geom_vline() function add lines to your previous plot for the 1946 introduction of the wP vaccine and the 1996 switch to aP vaccine (see example in the hint below). What do you notice?

```{r}
ggplot(cdc) +
  aes(Year, Cases) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1946, col = "blue", lty = 2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1996, col = "red", lty = 2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 2020, col = "gray", lty = 2) +
  labs(x="Year", y="Cases")
```
After the roll out of the wP vaccine in 1946, cases steadily declined. After the switch to the aP vaccine, the cases could be going up because of better testing, less vaccinations, and vaccine resistance. The aP vaccine also weins off faster than the wP vaccine.

